## 4.1 MOCK STRATEGY GUIDE
<!-- Integrated: 2026-01-04 -->

### Core Principle: Mock External I/O, Execute Internal Logic

**Problem:** Over-mocking (mocking internal flow-refs) breaks business logic and causes empty payload cascade.

**Solution:** Only mock external connectors. Let internal flow-refs execute to preserve flow integrity.

### ✅ MOCK (External I/O)
```
- solace:publish, solace:ack, solace:queue-listener
- http:request
- salesforce:upsert, salesforce:query
- db:select, db:insert, db:update
- jms:publish, vm:publish
- file:write, file:read
- email:send
```

### ✅ EXECUTE (Internal Logic)
```
- flow-ref to business flows
- flow-ref to utility flows (tracing, common)
- ee:transform (DataWeave)
- set-variable, set-payload
- choice, foreach, until-successful
- logger, error-handler
```

### ✅ SPY (Verify Without Blocking)
```
- ee:transform (to assert DataWeave output)
- flow-ref (to verify call count)
```

### Example: Good Mock Strategy
```xml
<!-- ✅ GOOD: Only mock connectors -->
<munit:behavior>
    <munit-tools:mock-when processor="solace:publish">
        <munit-tools:with-attributes>
            <munit-tools:with-attribute whereValue="{DOC_ID}" attributeName="doc:id"/>
        </munit-tools:with-attributes>
    </munit-tools:mock-when>
    
    <munit-tools:mock-when processor="http:request">
        <munit-tools:with-attributes>
            <munit-tools:with-attribute whereValue="{DOC_ID}" attributeName="doc:id"/>
        </munit-tools:with-attributes>
        <munit-tools:then-return>
            <munit-tools:payload value='#[MunitTools::getResourceAsString("test-data/response.json")]'/>
        </munit-tools:then-return>
    </munit-tools:mock-when>
    
    <!-- NO flow-ref mocks - let business logic execute naturally -->
</munit:behavior>
```

**Why:** Internal flow-refs need to execute for DataWeave transformations to work correctly. Mocking them causes empty payloads and variable loss.

