## 13. SCENARIO-BASED TEST GENERATION

> **Think-First**: Understand flow logic → Design scenarios → Generate tests

### Flow Logic Analysis Pattern (Phase 2)

For each flow, document:
```
FLOW: {flow-name}
  Transforms:
    - doc:id {id}: Sets vars.{name} = {logic}
  Choices:
    - When {condition} → {action}
    - Otherwise → {default}
  Foreach:
    - Collection: {expression}
    - Per item: {action}
  Errors:
    - Catches {type} → {handling}
```

### Scenario Design Pattern (Phase 3)

For each test scenario:
```
SCENARIO: {descriptive-name}
  Purpose: {what behavior is being tested}
  Input: {file reference or inline}
  Mocks: {connector} returns {response}
  Expected:
    - vars.{name} == {value}
    - {branch} taken/skipped
    - {processor} called N times
```

### Scenario Types

| Type | When to Use | Example |
|------|-------------|---------|
| Happy Path | Always | Valid input, all success |
| Branch A | Has choice | Input triggers first branch |
| Branch B | Has choice | Input triggers second branch |
| Error | Has error handler | Trigger connector error |
| Partial Failure | Bulk operations | Mix of success/failure |
| Empty Collection | Has foreach | Empty input array |

### Scenario-to-Test Mapping

Each scenario becomes one test:
```xml
<!-- SCENARIO: Contact Upsert Success -->
<munit:test name="test-contact-upsert-success" description="Scenario: Contact path with valid data">
    <munit:behavior>
        <!-- Mocks from scenario -->
    </munit:behavior>
    <munit:execution>
        <!-- Input from scenario -->
    </munit:execution>
    <munit:validation>
        <!-- Expected assertions from scenario -->
        <munit-tools:assert-that expression="#[vars.sfdcObjectType]" is="#[MunitTools::equalTo('Contact')]"/>
        <munit-tools:verify-call processor="flow-ref" times="#[1]"><!-- contact-path taken --></munit-tools:verify-call>
        <munit-tools:verify-call processor="flow-ref" times="#[0]"><!-- account-path skipped --></munit-tools:verify-call>
    </munit:validation>
</munit:test>
```

---

## 14. PRE-COMMIT CHECKLIST

**Schema & Syntax:**
- [ ] All doc:ids extracted from source XML
- [ ] No doc:name on structural elements
- [ ] xmlns:doc namespace declared
- [ ] Salesforce writes use output directive
- [ ] XML payloads use `encoding="UTF-8" mediaType="application/xml"`

**Behavior Verification:**
- [ ] Spies verify variable values (not just notNullValue)
- [ ] Output compared to expected files
- [ ] Each choice branch has a test
- [ ] Foreach iteration count verified
- [ ] Partial failure scenarios tested
- [ ] Critical connector calls verified (ack, publish)

**Security:**
- [ ] No credentials in test files

---

## 15. EVIDENCE-BOUND OUTPUTS

Every testing decision must cite its source:

| Decision Type | Evidence Format |
|---------------|----------------|
| Mock inclusion | "Mocking {connector} at doc:id {id} — Flow XML line {N}" |
| Scenario design | "Testing {branch} — Choice condition at doc:id {id}" |
| Assertion value | "Asserting vars.{name} == {value} — Transform at doc:id {id} sets this" |
| Assumption | "⚠️ ASSUMPTION: {value} — Flow logic unclear on '{detail}'" |
| Gap entry | "GAP-{N}: Unable to confirm {detail} (asked {X} times)" |

> If you cannot cite a source, flag as assumption. Never present assumptions as confirmed decisions.

---

## 16. TREE OF THOUGHT (Ambiguous Decisions)

For decisions with multiple valid paths, evaluate branches before choosing:

```
<thinking>
Branch A: [Option] — fits because [evidence]. Risk: [risk].
Branch B: [Option] — fits because [evidence]. Risk: [risk].
Best path: [chosen] — Confidence: {HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW}
</thinking>
```

**Common ambiguous decisions in MUnit testing:**
- Mock flow-ref vs let it execute naturally
- Spy before-call vs after-call for assertion placement
- Full payload comparison vs field-level assertions
- Error test with expectedErrorType vs try-catch validation

---

## 17. FEW-SHOT PATTERNS

### Schema Compliance

❌ BAD - doc:name on structural elements:
```xml
<munit:behavior doc:name="Setup">
<munit:execution doc:name="Run">
```

✅ GOOD - no doc:name on structural elements:
```xml
<munit:behavior>
<munit:execution>
```

**Why**: doc:name on structural elements causes 52+ XML schema errors.

### Mock Strategy

❌ BAD - Mocking internal flow-refs:
```xml
<munit-tools:mock-when processor="flow-ref">
    <munit-tools:then-return>
        <munit-tools:payload value="#[payload]"/>
    </munit-tools:then-return>
</munit-tools:mock-when>
```

✅ GOOD - Only mock external connectors:
```xml
<munit-tools:mock-when processor="http:request">
    <munit-tools:with-attributes>
        <munit-tools:with-attribute whereValue="{DOC_ID}" attributeName="doc:id"/>
    </munit-tools:with-attributes>
    <munit-tools:then-return>
        <munit-tools:payload value='#[MunitTools::getResourceAsString("test-data/response.json")]'/>
    </munit-tools:then-return>
</munit-tools:mock-when>
```

**Why**: Mocking flow-refs causes empty payload cascade and breaks business logic.

